<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1607">1607</a>–<a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Stanley, 7th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, was an English royalist commander and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-mann">Lord of Mann</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> he fought for Charles I and used the Isle of Man as a royalist base. After joining Charles II's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he was captured following the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> and beheaded at Bolton in October 1651.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-stanley-7th-earl-of-derby.jpg" alt="James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sir Anthony Van Dyck (died 1641) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Stanley,_7th_Earl_of_Derby_by_Sir_Anthony_Van_Dyck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born at Knowsley Hall, near Lathom House, on 31 January 1607, the eldest son of William Stanley, 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/earl-of-derby">Earl of Derby</a>, and Elizabeth de Vere, a daughter of Edward de Vere, 17th Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>; through his father's mother he descended from Henry VII. Known as Lord Strange until he inherited the earldom in 1642, he was elected Member of Parliament for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a> in 1625, made a Knight of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> at the coronation of Charles I in 1626, served with his father as Lord Lieutenant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>, and in 1627 became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-of-mann">Lord of Mann</a>. On 26 June 1626 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlotte-stanley-countess-of-derby">Charlotte de La Trémoille</a>, a granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William I, Prince of Orange</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He had kept out of the disputes between King and Parliament, but in 1642 took the king's side. Charles, said to be jealous of his power and royal descent, discouraged his plan to secure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> and summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottingham">Nottingham</a>. Derby then failed to take Manchester, was beaten at Chowbent and Lowton Moor, failed before Bolton and Lancaster Castle in 1643 and, after beating off Sir William Brereton at Warrington, was defeated at the Battle of Whalley. In the summer of 1644 he joined Prince Rupert's northern campaign, which relieved the Siege of Lathom House, defended by his wife, and took Bolton with much bloodshed in the Bolton Massacre.</p><p>After Marston Moor he held the Isle of Man for the king and sheltered royalist fugitives. His rule there kept order and encouraged trade, but he imprisoned opponents and roused long agitation by abolishing tenant-right. In July 1649 he refused the terms offered by Henry Ireton. He landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> on 15 August 1651 to support Charles II and was badly wounded in his defeat at the Battle of Wigan Lane on 25 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> he went with Charles to Boscobel House, but was captured near Nantwich, tried by court-martial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> on 29 September and condemned for treason. Parliament rejected his appeal for pardon, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> supported it. He was beheaded on 15 October 1651 at the market cross in Churchgate, Bolton, chosen for his part in the massacre there, and buried at Ormskirk. Clarendon called him a man of great honour and clear courage. He left in manuscript A Discourse Concerning the Government of the Isle of Man.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby</b>, held it for Charles I, and his wife, Charlotte de la Tremoille, defended it until 1651.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-mann">Lordship of Mann</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Knowsley Hall, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Bolton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.44,-2.84&amp;year=1635" aria-label="Knowsley Hall on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4487 326 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Stanley, 7th Earl of Derby"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4937 596h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4937 596h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.44,-2.84&amp;year=1635">What was here in 1635? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
